    {
      "heading": {
        "text": "UNIT 100 — SULFUR HANDLING AND MELTING",
        "level": 1
      },
      "level": 1,
      "paragraphs": [],
      "tables": []
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "text": "Process Narrative",
        "level": 2
      },
      "level": 2,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Molten elemental sulfur is received from road tankers at 135–145°C into insulated, steam-traced storage tanks (capacity: minimum 7-day supply at 100 t/d nameplate = 9,541 t minimum storage). The storage system consists of two tanks of equal size operating in duty/standby configuration with a common suction header. Each tank is fitted with electric immersion heaters and/or steam coil heating to maintain sulfur at 138–143°C (operating band). Sulfur vapour pressure at 143°C is approximately 0.08 barg — tanks are designed for 0.5 barg working pressure (atmospheric + vapour space).",
          "style": "Normal"
        },
        {
          "text": "Molten sulfur is pumped from storage via a dedicated duty/standby pump set (gear pumps, ATEX-rated, heated casing) through 4-inch insulated and electrically heat-traced carbon steel pipe to the sulfur filter unit. The filter removes ash, carbon, and solid particulates >25 micron to protect vanadium catalyst. After filtration, a Coriolis mass flow meter (FT-101) measures and controls the sulfur feed rate to the furnace via a heated modulating control valve (FCV-101).",
          "style": "Normal"
        }
      ],
      "tables": []
    },
