    {
      "heading": {
        "text": "UNIT 200 — SULFUR COMBUSTION AND WASTE HEAT BOILER",
        "level": 1
      },
      "level": 1,
      "paragraphs": [],
      "tables": []
    },
    {
      "heading": {
        "text": "Process Narrative",
        "level": 2
      },
      "level": 2,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Filtered molten sulfur is atomised through 2 × 100% stainless steel spray guns into the horizontal cylindrical sulfur furnace. The furnace burner zone is refractory-lined (castable high-alumina refractory, service temperature 1,400°C). Dried process air, compressed by the main air blower, enters the furnace through a windbox and annular air register. Sulfur auto-ignites at approximately 250°C; at normal operating temperatures of 1,000–1,050°C, combustion is complete within the furnace residence time (approximately 3 seconds at design flow).",
          "style": "Normal"
        },
        {
          "text": "The process gas exits the furnace at 1,000–1,100°C into the Waste Heat Boiler (WHB), a fire-tube boiler design with process gas on the tube side. The WHB cools the gas from ~1,050°C to ~420°C while generating HP steam at 40 barg/400°C. A steam drum integral to the WHB package maintains water level; a three-element level control (LIC-201) regulates BFW admission.",
          "style": "Normal"
        }
      ],
      "tables": []
    },
